From fca5e8143e4bc418cd42232e02260eeaf2e8958f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ivy Evans <ivy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 02:25:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] Controller: keep the stick's movement vector inside the
 unit circle

GetJoystickBuffer's replacement emits the stick as a direction times the
rescaled deflection, but the clamp to 1 applied only to the deflection
and the direction kept the raw vector's length. A thumbstick reports its
corners at up to sqrt(2), and with Vector::Normalize skipped for the
analog path that length reached the character as speed: measured at
1.41 from the engine's own movement fields (ud=-999, lr=999) whenever a
flick landed in a corner, which is also where a quick tilt was reported
to send the character the wrong way. Scale the unit vector instead, as
ReadNativeStickK1 already does for the diagnostics.
---
 CHANGELOG.md                               | 14 ++++++++++++++
 docs/controller-native-path.md             | 14 ++++++++++++++
 src/controller-native/K1NativeJoystick.cpp | 14 ++++++++++----
 3 files changed, 38 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 11cd234..0168362 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -117,6 +117,20 @@ proxy, and it applies the executable changes above as well.
 
 ## [Unreleased]
 
+- **Controller: the stick's movement vector stays inside the unit circle** (2026-10-15;
+  `src/controller-native/K1NativeJoystick.cpp`, `FillNativeJoystickBufferK1`;
+  `docs/controller-native-path.md`, "Deadzone"). Reported from play on a ROG Ally X under
+  Proton: a quick tilt of the left stick, forward then hard right, ran the character faster,
+  and at times the wrong way. Read from the pad log (`debug-logs=2`): the engine's own
+  movement fields reached `ud=-999 lr=999`, a vector of 1.41, whenever the flick landed in a
+  corner of the stick's gate, against 1.00 for a straight push. The buffer's replacement
+  clamped the deflection to 1 but scaled the raw direction, which keeps the corner's length,
+  and the analog path skips `Vector::Normalize`, so that length became speed. It now scales
+  the unit vector, as `ReadNativeStickK1` already did for the diagnostics. **Built on Linux
+  and played: the speed is constant across the flick and the reversal was not seen again in
+  the same test.** The reversal's own mechanism was not read out of the engine; a vector longer
+  than 1 is the only input to its heading code that changed.
+
 - **Controller: A on a message box no longer clicks the button behind it** (2026-10-13;
   `src/controller-native/K1NativeJoystick.cpp`, `GuardPanelEchoK1`;
   `reverse-engineering/retained-xbox-gui-events.md`). Reported by the maintainer on Party
diff --git a/docs/controller-native-path.md b/docs/controller-native-path.md
index e5abaad..ae2d742 100644
--- a/docs/controller-native-path.md
+++ b/docs/controller-native-path.md
@@ -474,6 +474,20 @@ use is a third threshold, `K1_ACTIVITY_STICK`, 0.35.
 `K1_STICK_DEADZONE` and `K1_CAMERA_DEADZONE` are named constants, tunable
 without touching logic.
 
+The rescaled vector is the stick's **unit vector times the rescaled
+deflection**, so it never exceeds the unit circle. *Corrected 2026-10-15:* until
+then the deflection was clamped to 1 but the direction kept the raw vector's
+length, and a thumbstick reports its corners at up to sqrt(2); with the engine's
+`Vector::Normalize` skipped for the analog path (below), that length reached the
+character as speed. Measured from the engine's own movement fields with
+`debug-logs=2` on a ROG Ally X under Proton: `move ud=-999 lr=999`, a vector of
+1.41, whenever a flick landed in a corner, against 1.00 for a straight push; a
+gradual tilt rarely reaches a corner, which is why the extra speed showed as a
+"quick tilt" fault. Reported together with the character heading the wrong way
+on such a flick; with the vector bounded the speed is constant and the
+reversal has not been seen again, though its mechanism was not read out of the
+engine.
+
 ## The camera
 
 **Working, playtest-confirmed.** The right stick turns the camera through the
diff --git a/src/controller-native/K1NativeJoystick.cpp b/src/controller-native/K1NativeJoystick.cpp
index 4bf35b8..f1f97cd 100644
--- a/src/controller-native/K1NativeJoystick.cpp
+++ b/src/controller-native/K1NativeJoystick.cpp
@@ -1398,13 +1398,19 @@ void FillNativeJoystickBufferK1(int deviceIndex, void* outBuffer)
     g_stick.analogMagnitude = 0.0f;
     g_stick.lastBufferTick = GetTickCount();
     if (magnitude > K1_STICK_DEADZONE) {
+        // The direction is the raw vector's; the length is the rescaled
+        // deflection, clamped so that the corners of a square gate, which a
+        // thumbstick reports at up to sqrt(2), stay on the unit circle. With
+        // the engine's Normalize skipped for the analog path, anything longer
+        // than 1 reaches the character as that much extra speed.
+        const float unitX = nx / magnitude;
+        const float unitY = ny / magnitude;
         if (magnitude > 1.0f) {
-            magnitude = 1.0f;               // the corners of a square gate
+            magnitude = 1.0f;
         }
         const float scaled = (magnitude - K1_STICK_DEADZONE) / (1.0f - K1_STICK_DEADZONE);
-        const float factor = (scaled / magnitude) * K1_AXIS_FULL_SCALE;
-        const std::int32_t ex = static_cast<std::int32_t>(nx * factor);
-        const std::int32_t ey = static_cast<std::int32_t>(ny * factor);
+        const std::int32_t ex = static_cast<std::int32_t>(unitX * scaled * K1_AXIS_FULL_SCALE);
+        const std::int32_t ey = static_cast<std::int32_t>(unitY * scaled * K1_AXIS_FULL_SCALE);
         if (ex != 0) {
             emit(DIJOFS_X_OFFSET, ex);
         }
